Library Functions and System calls
----------------------------------

Library functions are supported by the compiler and part of std libraries. 

	Ex : printf(), scanf(), fopen(), fgetc(), malloc(),...

	Library function execute in user mode. 


System Calls are supported by the kernel and provide a way for user programs to request kernel services. 

	Ex : open(), read(), ...

man command and manual sections 

	Example : 

		$ man man 

		output : 

			Interface to the man reference manual 

		The table below shows the section number of the manual followed by the types of pages they contain : 

		1   Executable programs or shell commands
		2   System calls (functions provided by the kernel)
		3   Library calls (functions within program libraries)
		4   Special files (usually found in /dev)
		5   File formats and conventions, e.g., /etc/passwd
		6   Games
		7   Miscellaneous (macro packages and conventions)
    		    e.g., man(7), groff(7), man-pages(7)
		8   System administration commands (usually only for root)
		9   Kernel routines (non-standard)

	A manual page consists of several sections such as NAME, SYNOPSIS, DESCRIPTION, RETURN VALUE, etc.


Relationship Between Library Functions and System Calls

	Library functions internally call system calls to access kernel services. 

	Example mapping 

	printf()        fgetc()         fopen()        // User mode
	   |               |               |
	   |               |               |
	 [              System Calls              ]
	   |               |               |
	 write()         read()          open()        // Kernel mode


User space and kernel Space Interfaction 
	
		[ User Application ]
        		|
		[ System Call Interface ]  <----- Interface between user mode & kernel mode
          		|
     		 [ Kernel Mode ]

	* User applications cannot access hardware directly.
	* System calls act as a controlled gateway to the kernel.


